# two_forecasts_at_one_instant_are_one_ground
#
# The level drops and the temperature rises, and BOTH begin at 13:00. One boundary, one world
# change, one ground — holding both.
#
# A BOUNDARY IS AN INSTANT, NOT A FORECAST, and forking once per forecast got that wrong in a
# way nothing would have reported. Two grounds were made with the SAME name, since a ground is
# named for the instant it opens at, and the first was classified with a period running from
# the instant to itself — which holds at no instant at all. A reader standing at half past one
# was handed NO ground: not a stale world, not a wrong world, nothing, and the agent went
# blind past the boundary.
#
# EACH RETRACT READS THE GROUND STANDING BEFORE THE INSTANT, so the two supersede in parallel
# rather than one seeing the other's work. Each names only its own predicate, so the dip's
# level and the heat's temperature both survive into the one ground.
#
# `two_stretches_in_one_scope_are_two_grounds` is the other arrangement of the same two
# forecasts — apart in time, three grounds, the diffs accumulating — and
# `a_forecast_that_says_nothing_new_is_no_period` is the third: a forecast whose retract and
# add net out to the state before it hashes the same, and is no period.
#
# NOTHING IS IN ONE SCOPE HERE AS FAR AS THE FILL IS CONCERNED, and that is the point of
# saying it: a scope names WHICH imaginarium this is and does not cut what crosses into it.
# Narrowing the fill per scope was built, measured at 10.4 ms against 0.6 on a pass costing
# over a second, and refused — the lean set fails by returning an EMPTY RESULT rather than an
# error, and fewer graphs is the same move under another name.
#
# The clock stands at 2026-01-01T12:00:00Z.

@prefix : <http://example.org/test#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix planning: <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .
@prefix ground: <http://example.org/orexis/graph/ground/> .

GRAPH :ontology {
  orexis:PredictionGraph rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph .
  orexis:StateGraph rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph .
}

GRAPH :world {
  :keeper a orexis:Agent .
  :tank1 a :Tank .
}

GRAPH :sensed {
  :tank1 :level 20 .
  :tank1 :temperature 20 .
}

#  THE LEVEL DROPS — and takes the standing reading with it.
GRAPH :dip {
  :tank1 :level 5 .
}

#  THE TEMPERATURE RISES AT THE SAME INSTANT. It says nothing about the level, and its
#  retract names only its own predicate, so both changes stand in the one ground.
GRAPH :heat {
  :tank1 :temperature 40 .
}

GRAPH :catalogue {
  :catalogue a orexis:CatalogueGraph .
  :catalogue a orexis:Graph .
  :dip a orexis:Graph .
  :dip a orexis:PredictionGraph .
  :dip dcterms:temporal [ a dcterms:PeriodOfTime ; orexis:start "2026-01-01T13:00:00+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ] .
  :dip orexis:beliefsOf :keeper .
  :dip orexis:retracts """PREFIX : <http://example.org/test#>
      DELETE { GRAPH $state { :tank1 :level ?l } }
      WHERE  { GRAPH $state { :tank1 :level ?l } }""" .
  :heat a orexis:Graph .
  :heat a orexis:PredictionGraph .
  :heat dcterms:temporal [ a dcterms:PeriodOfTime ; orexis:start "2026-01-01T13:00:00+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ] .
  :heat orexis:beliefsOf :keeper .
  :heat orexis:retracts """PREFIX : <http://example.org/test#>
      DELETE { GRAPH $state { :tank1 :temperature ?t } }
      WHERE  { GRAPH $state { :tank1 :temperature ?t } }""" .
  :ontology a orexis:Graph .
  :ontology a orexis:OntologyGraph .
  :ontology a orexis:PublicGraph .
  :sensed a orexis:Graph .
  :sensed a orexis:StateGraph .
  :sensed orexis:beliefsOf :keeper .
  :world a orexis:Graph .
  :world a orexis:PublicGraph .
}
